WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.060
- Turns out, Americans are pretty good

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.660
at guessing your race just
by hearing your voice.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.440
It's called, linguistic profiling,

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.640
and we all do it.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.450
There are certain auditory cues

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:18.000
that give us subtle hints
about who we're talking to.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.720
A lot of black American's
speak what's referred to as,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.603
AAVE, or African American
Vernacular English.

00:00:24.603 --> 00:00:29.290
- AAVE is non-rotic meaning,
that like a British accent,

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.100
there are no "R" sounds
at the end of words

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.310
or at the end of syllables.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:35.650
- John Flemming is a
speech and dialect coach

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.740
for movies and video games.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.380
- Generally speaking,
there's the "th" sounds,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.010
the "tha, tha" and, "the the,"
get replaced with something.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.900
Generally a "T," or a "D."

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.430
There's a few other vowels and things,

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.010
but it kind of keeps going and going,

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.410
and it gets kind of, into the weeds a bit.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.360
- When it comes to being
on the phone though,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.300
black people will often
try to sound more white.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.730
- Uh, yes, Mister Ribbon I would love

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.390
to have the opportunity to sit with you

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.960
to discuss some of our products.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.720
It's a form of code-switching.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.530
Or in this case, alternating between

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:06.530
two different dialects.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.730
- He tried to grab me by my collar, right.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:09.970
That's when I ---

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:10.830
Uh, good morning.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.630
Customer service, my name is Philip.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.080
- Black people talking white

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.490
is having a real moment
in media right now.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.040
"Sorry To Bother You", is a
movie about a black salesman

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.400
who's career only takes off

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.280
when he starts talking white.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.920
- You wanna make some money
here, use your white voice.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.003
Hey, Mister Kramer, this is
Langston from Regal View.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.820
- [Manny] And "BlacKkKlansman",
is a movie about

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.450
a black detective who infiltrates the KKK

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.290
by pretending to be white over the phone.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.270
- God bless White America.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:40.920
- The idea is to speak in what's called

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.300
the standard accent.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.380
- The standard accent is the one spoken

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.120
by the majority group, or the
socially advantaged group.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.696
- [Manny] Olivia Kang is
a psychologist at Harvard.

00:01:49.696 --> 00:01:51.820
She's working on a project that explores

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.490
the hidden biases we hold.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.690
- If you speak with a standard accent,

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.808
you're judged as being more intelligent,

00:01:57.808 --> 00:02:00.870
more competent, more
credible, more hireable.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:04.370
Now, having a regional accent,
or a non-standard accent,

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:05.900
now you're not getting those advantages.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.050
You're seen as less
credible, or less hireable.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:10.860
- These biases are implicit.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.550
This isn't Charlotesville
tiki-torch racism,

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.350
it's much more discreet than that.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:18.130
- The most basic way of
understanding implicit bias

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.760
is by thinking about the
associations we make.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.400
If two things occur
together over and over again

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.160
in our experience, we link them together.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.790
- Salt and pepper, day and
night, bread and butter.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.090
These types of associations are helpful,

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.670
but there are some that aren't.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.350
Now, if I say the word, genius,

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:37.610
you're probably thinking of some

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.600
collectors edition white dude.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.473
Now, if I say the word criminal...

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.530
- Voices aren't just sounds.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.060
In a lot of ways they're auditory faces.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.320
So, when you hear a voice,
you can in some sense

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.290
piece together what the
person on the other end

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.040
of the phone, can look like.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.350
Roughly how old they are, their gender,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.170
where they come from.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:01.360
But, the interesting thing is that

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.240
you can also form
impressions about character.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.220
So, how intelligent someone is,

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.210
how competent, how
likable, how trustworthy.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:13.200
And, these impressions
can be flawed, right?

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.600
A lot of these things might have

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.810
a basis in your implicit biases,

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:18.880
or things that you've heard,

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.900
portrayals you've seen on television.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.020
And so, when people are doing something,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.810
like conducting a voice interview,

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.150
often the implicit biases
they have about voice

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.608
can influence the decisions
that they're making.

00:03:32.608 --> 00:03:35.730
- Every black American is bilingual.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.113
We speak street vernacular,
and we speak job interview.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.970
- In order to combat some
of these implicit biases,

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.440
black people, myself
included, try to sound

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.010
as white as possible.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:51.980
I sound like Bill Gates
impersonating your mailman.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:52.930
Why hello there.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.010
I'm wondering if there are
any positions available.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:56.570
Oh no, I don't mind waiting.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.660
But, does changing your
voice actually work.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.490
A study in 2001 found that landlords

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:03.370
would make racist snap judgements

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.470
to callers with certain dialects.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.870
Compared with whites,
blacks were less likely

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:10.180
to get call backs, less likely to be told

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.700
there was an apartment available,

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.330
and more likely to get
their credit questioned.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.180
However, blacks who
code-switched fared better

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.950
than those who didn't.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.500
Code-switching helps in other areas too.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.390
- Actually Don Lemon
is a very good example,

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:26.490
because to become this
main host that he is,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.070
he's talked about how he had to change -

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.640
because he's from New
Orleans - and he consciously

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.080
had to change his accent for
the sake of moving up at CNN.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.000
- The president of the
United States is racist.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.240
- Instead of changing the way we speak,

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.530
maybe we should change the way we listen.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.860
I might be able to tell
your race from your voice,

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.920
but that doesn't tell me
anything about your character.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.229
There's tons of black mailmen.

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:50.931
- [Delisa] Too many.

00:04:50.931 --> 00:04:52.379
My dad is a black mailman.

00:04:52.379 --> 00:04:55.540
So don't be talking about
BS like that, 'cuz my dad

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.860
will run up on you real quick.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:59.060
- I don't want no smoke.

